# Copy this to .env and fill in your values.
#
# EVERYTHING IN THIS FILE IS PUBLIC.
# Vite inlines every VITE_* variable into the JavaScript bundle served to every
# visitor. A VITE_ variable is not a secret and cannot be made one. Server-side
# secrets live in Supabase Function Secrets — see docs/GOOGLE_CALENDAR_SYNC.md.

# ── Supabase ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Get these from: https://app.supabase.com → Project Settings → API
# The anon key is public by design; row-level security is what protects the data.
# Leave both unset to run the site in demo mode (no backend, seeded sample data).
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-id.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key-here

# ── Google Calendar sync ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The frontend needs NOTHING here. The whole OAuth flow runs in Edge Functions:
# the coach portal calls google-oauth-start, which returns Google's consent URL,
# and Google redirects back to google-oauth-callback. The browser never sees a
# client ID, a client secret, an auth code, or a token.
#
# NEVER put any of the following in this file, and NEVER prefix them with VITE_:
#
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET       <- prefixing this with VITE_ publishes it to every
#                                 visitor and hands over every coach's calendar
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID
#   GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI
#   TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY       <- decrypts every stored Google refresh token
#   ALLOWED_ORIGINS
#   SITE_URL
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY  <- bypasses all row-level security
#
# They belong in Supabase -> Project Settings -> Edge Functions -> Secrets
# (or `supabase secrets set NAME=value`). Setup: docs/GOOGLE_CALENDAR_SYNC.md

# ── Booking (Edge Function secrets — NOT here, NOT prefixed VITE_) ──────────
# The booking flow reads these server-side. Same rule as above: every one of
# them is a Function Secret and none of them may ever appear in this file.
#
#   RESEND_API_KEY             <- sends every booking email. booking-notify is
#                                 inert without it and leaves the queue unsent
#                                 rather than pretending it delivered.
#   BOOKING_FROM_EMAIL         <- optional. Defaults to
#                                 'Axis Training Systems <noreply@axistrainingsystems.com>';
#                                 the domain must be verified in Resend.
#   SITE_URL                   <- the origin used to build the "manage your
#                                 booking" link in emails. Wrong here means
#                                 every client gets a dead link.
#   RATE_LIMIT_SALT            <- optional. Salts the digest of the caller's IP
#                                 before it is counted, so the rate-limit table
#                                 never holds an address in the clear.
#
# ── Accounts and invitations ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Also nothing here. Sign-in is configured in the Supabase DASHBOARD, not in
# this file, and getting one setting wrong there is the most common way the
# whole flow silently fails:
#
#   Authentication → Providers → Google
#     Client ID and secret from Google Cloud. Supabase's own callback
#     (https://<ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback) must be an authorised
#     redirect URI on the Google side.
#
#   Authentication → URL Configuration → Redirect URLs
#     Every origin the app is served from, WITH the callback path:
#       https://axistrainingsystems.com/auth/callback
#       http://localhost:5173/auth/callback
#     A missing entry here looks like a working sign-in that produces no
#     session. /auth/callback says so rather than spinning.
#
# invite-send reuses RESEND_API_KEY, BOOKING_FROM_EMAIL and SITE_URL from the
# booking system above — it needs no secrets of its own. Without a Resend key an
# invitation is still created and still effective (the email match does not need
# the mail to arrive); the admin panel shows the link to send by hand.
#
#   supabase functions deploy invite-send    # verify_jwt stays ON
#
# Full setup and the migration warning: docs/AUTH.md

# The booking page itself needs nothing beyond the two VITE_ variables above.
# Slots, prices and durations are all computed server-side (migration 009) and
# the browser is never told how long a call is — it asks which service.
#
# Deploy the booking functions:
#   supabase functions deploy booking-availability --no-verify-jwt
#   supabase functions deploy booking-create       --no-verify-jwt
#   supabase functions deploy booking-manage       --no-verify-jwt
#   supabase functions deploy booking-update
#   supabase functions deploy booking-notify
#
# booking-notify then needs a schedule, or nothing is ever sent — the queue
# fills and sits there. See the header of supabase/functions/booking-notify.
